---
// Method sheet — the ill-tempered week (2026-11-09). The six parts of the method-sheet duty (werkgruppe spec §3.5).
// Method in full: docs/design/2026-11-08-common-time-method.md.
import MethodSheet from '@/components/experiments/MethodSheet.astro'
import { clock, readAlbums } from '@/lib/ill-tempered-week/albums'

const REPO = 'https://github.com/frankbueltge/frankbueltge.de'
const link = 'underline decoration-line underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-fg'
const albums = readAlbums()
const first = albums[0]!
// The newest complete album (the growing week only until one exists).
const album = albums.filter((a) => a.complete).at(-1) ?? albums.at(-1)!
const minutes = Object.values(album.pieces).reduce((s, p) => s + p!.seconds, 0)
---

<MethodSheet title="Method sheet — the ill-tempered week" workHref="/ill-tempered-week" workLabel="To the work">
  <section class="sheet-reveal mb-10">
    <h2 class="kicker mb-2">What this is</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed">
      A week of the web’s attention, composed. Each ISO week of Common Ground becomes an album, a <em>Contrapunctus</em> numbered by the
      week, of three pieces in three idioms ({clock(minutes)} in {album.title}). The title alludes twice to Bach: to the
      <em>Well-Tempered Clavier</em>, here a week in bad temper and in just intonation, not tempered at all; and to the numbered
      <em>Contrapunctus</em> of the <em>Art of Fugue</em>, whose last breaks off mid-phrase as every edition breaks off at its
      newest day. The claim is narrow: which headline is heard, how far a mass of strings reaches harmony and how full a rhythm is,
      each follows a written rule from committed day files, and a test holds every album to the files it names. The music adds no
      finding to Common Ground; it lets a week be heard from where the attention was not.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">1. Sources &amp; licences</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        Common Ground’s day files, open data (CC0), in <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/tree/main/src/data/trending`}>src/data/trending</a>,
        counted by the house’s one count into the thirteen fields of the map, with each field’s terms from the newest Ducking round
        checked by the newest day (<a class={link} href={`${REPO}/tree/main/src/data/ducking/rounds`}>src/data/ducking/rounds</a>).
      </li>
      <li>
        Instruments: the VSCO 2 Community Edition by Versilian Studios (recorded by Sam Gossner and Simon Dalzell), CC0, version
        1.1.0; every recorded note is tuned exactly, to just intonation on A. Voices: Kokoro-82M, Apache 2.0, run locally. No music
        model: nothing is generated by a system trained on music.
      </li>
      <li>
        The albums: audio (AAC) in <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/tree/main/public/ill-tempered-week`}>public/ill-tempered-week</a>
        and records in <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/tree/main/src/data/ill-tempered-week`}>src/data/ill-tempered-week</a>, CC BY 4.0
        with the rest of the lab’s works; the renderer in
        <a class={link} href={`${REPO}/tree/main/scripts/ill-tempered-week`}>scripts/ill-tempered-week</a>, Apache 2.0.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">2. Cadence</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Daily, after the ledger. When a new day file lands, <em>Overlooked</em> is pressed again from the week’s days so far and replaces the edition
      before; when the week’s seventh day has landed, <em>Unison</em> and <em>Turn</em> are pressed too and the album is complete. A
      complete album is never pressed again. The press starts as soon as the ledger has pushed its day file; if that start fails, a daily fallback run presses the edition later, often hours later.
      The first album is {first.title}, the week of the work’s start.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">3. Processing</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Overlooked</strong> (chamber ensemble). Each day, the speakable headline with the least attention on its
        own platform (its place among the platform’s signals that day: points, posts, searches, dollars, views, editions), among
        all thirteen fields and from a field not heard before that week; on a tie, the field the day named least. Said once in its
        field’s Ducking voice, answered by its field’s instrument with its speech melody (each syllable’s spoken pitch, its interval to
        the line’s median widened by half, on the nearest note of the harmonic scale 8 to 15 of A1 = 55 Hz), then looped as a motif on a
        0.2 s pulse and taken up in canon. Earlier motifs sound on, 4 dB softer a day, and end with their day if the newest day
        no longer names their field. The day’s four most attended headlines murmur underneath, granulated.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Unison</strong> (strings, brass, timpani). Every war signal is a glissando: its section by its attention,
        its first pitch from its headline as spoken, its entry at its publication hour. Each day the glissandi glide towards the
        nearest tone of A’s harmonic chord (4:5:6:7) by the share of the day’s war signals naming its most named story. From 30 % on,
        brass and a timpani roll take up the chord and the most attended war headline is said; after each peak a solo double bass
        plays the least attended one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Turn</strong> (electronics). Each platform family pulses as many beats a bar as its harmonic number
        (search 5, news 6, social 7, tech 9) and sings its vowel on that harmonic; as many beats sound as it had AI signals that day,
        spread evenly. A bar lasts 2.4 s times the square root of the mean count so far over the day’s count. On the first day with
        at least twice the mean of the days before, from the third day on, one bar sounds every beat and a silent bar carries the
        day’s most attended AI headline.
      </li>
      <li>
        Headlines are voiced as they stand: only what a voice cannot say stays out (web addresses, code, formulas, non-Latin script,
        emoji), the same rule as Ducking’s record.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">4. Limits of the method</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>Attention is a platform’s own ranking that day, not a reach measured across platforms; a headline at the bottom of a busy feed counts as overlooked.</li>
      <li>A story is a field term (Iran, Ukraine), not an event; two different events under one name count as one story, which is how the press’s agreement is measured.</li>
      <li>Speech melodies come from a synthetic voice reading the headline, not from a person; they are the voice’s intonation of the words, not the writer’s.</li>
      <li>The form of each piece is composed and fixed; the data decide what sounds within it, never the form itself.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">5. Compute footprint</h2>
    <p class="leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      Some minutes of CPU a night on the workflow’s runner, no paid service, no GPU. The runner keeps the voice model and the two
      dozen instruments the pieces play (about 1.2 GB of the library’s 3 GB) in its cache between nights. A complete album is
      about twelve megabytes of audio and a record of tens of kilobytes; because each day’s edition of <em>Overlooked</em>
      replaces the one before and the history keeps them all, the repository grows by about thirty megabytes a week.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-8">
    <h2 class="mb-2 font-semibold">6. Change log</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
      <li>2026-11-08 — begun as Common Time, a weekly pressing in one fixed template; never published.</li>
      <li>2026-11-09 — after probes in five forms and the owner’s verdict that the sound must come first, rebuilt as an album of three composed pieces with recorded instruments, and renamed <em>the ill-tempered week</em>. {first.title} pressed the same day.</li>
      <li>2026-11-09 — the title written in lowercase: in the house typeface a capital I and a lowercase l are the same stroke, and “Ill” read as the numeral III beside pieces numbered I to III.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</MethodSheet>
